  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 333 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nGusty winds & scattered rain showers are in store today \nassociated with a cold front moving through the region. \nSoutherly winds will quickly increase after sunrise with the \nstrongest winds peaking over 40 mph across southwest Minnesota \nthis morning. Elsewhere, gusts of 25-35 mph are expected from \nmid-morning into late afternoon. The gusty winds could lead to \noverachieving temperatures today where deeper mixing & some \ncompressional warming occurs immediately ahead of the cold \nfront his afternoon. Could see highs reach the low 70s across \nsouthwest Minnesota where the aforementioned strongest winds \nwill occur, while temperatures warm into the low to mid 60s \nelsewhere.\n\nA few isolated showers are possible across southern \nMinnesota this morning, but the main band of rain will develop \nacross western Minnesota during the early to mid- afternoon,\nspreading into eastern Minnesota 7 western Wisconsin through the\nlate afternoon & evening. Rainfall amounts will be light,\ngenerally 0.1-0.25\", but some locally heavier downpours could\noccur with the strongest showers. Just enough instability looks\nto be present this afternoon to generate a few rumbles of \nthunder, but widespread thunderstorms are not expected. The \nrainfall ends from west to east through the late afternoon 7 \nevening with most of the area dry by 10 PM.\n\nCooler & dry weather returns behind the front Sunday through\nMonday, with highs mainly in the low to mid 60s. Sunday night\nwill be the coolest period with lows falling into the mid 30s\nacross portions of easst-central Minnesota & western Wisconsin,\nbringing the potential for patchy frost in rural low-lying \nareas. Otherwise, the forecast remains quiet through next week \nas the main storm track stays north of the region. Temperatures \nwarm back above normal Tuesday through the end of the week, with\nhighs generally in the mid 60s to lower 70s.",
